{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-77237" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=-\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-77237" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},$ therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{3}}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value of $x.$ \n\n$$\nx=\\text{arcsin}(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=-{60}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nThis value is outside the required domain. To get a value inside the range, we add ${360}^{∘}.$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=x+{360}^{∘}=-{60}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}={300}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSo, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={300}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/assets/5fe064cdc2d97e3db894801f6f31a4679881efcf83564e24a1ea2559c15d2085.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{60}^{∘} \\\\  & ={240}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x={300}^{∘},{240}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=300\\cdot 240=72\\,000.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-77237" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f6c1fdf246bff5ae7680fa49922f6b12948c74816459e66df46444dda9aed443"} {"question" "q-77237" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-77237" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "28\\,800"] ["math" "36\\,000"] ["math" "3\\,600"] ["math" "72\\,000"] ["math" "7\\,200"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-77237" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "72\\,000"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-120532" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+3=-1-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-120532" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+3 & =-1-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x \\\\ 2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-4 \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},$ therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value: \n\n$$\nx=\\text{arcsin}(-\\frac{1}{2})=-{30}^{∘}\n$$\n\nThis value is outside the required domain. To get a value inside the range, we add ${360}^{∘}.$ \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=x+{360}^{∘}=-{30}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}={330}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSo,  our first solution is ${x}_{1}={330}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={30}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={30}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/assets/b3c523e882baf0b3a105e10131091e40de686b4c1483f46e7bdc235e30743a6d.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{30}^{∘} \\\\  & ={210}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x={210}^{∘},{330}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=210\\cdot 330=69\\,300.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-120532" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f6c1fdf246bff5ae7680fa49922f6b12948c74816459e66df46444dda9aed443"} {"question" "q-120532" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-120532" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "36\\,000"] ["math" "49\\,500"] ["math" "63\\,000"] ["math" "69\\,300"] ["math" "79\\,200"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-120532" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "69\\,300"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-338555" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The solution to the equation $4\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=3\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-1$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}$ is $x=${{field-1}}${}^{∘}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-338555" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =3\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-1.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value of $x:$\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-1)=-{90}^{∘}\n$$\n\nNote that $x=-{90}^{∘}$ is not in the given domain. To get a value inside the range, we add ${360}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-{90}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}={270}^{∘}\n$$\n\nAs this is a quadrantal angle, to find the remaining solutions, we use our knowledge of the sine function. We know that the sine function has its minimum values at\n\n$$\nx={270}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSince the domain $0\\le x<{360}^{∘}$ is one period, and we have already found one minimum value in this period, we conclude that \n$x={270}^{∘}$\nis the only solution." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-338555" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f6c1fdf246bff5ae7680fa49922f6b12948c74816459e66df46444dda9aed443"} {"question" "q-338555" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-338555" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "270"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-29004" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-29004" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},$ therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{3}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\text{arcsin}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the  first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/assets/4cbf970747a9eddbcffe6fd3ba46d13c43e5f17ca56e52747c797229dbc74d48.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot \\frac{2\\pi }{3}=\\frac{2{\\pi }^{2}}{9}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-29004" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f6c1fdf246bff5ae7680fa49922f6b12948c74816459e66df46444dda9aed443"} {"question" "q-29004" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-29004" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{2{\\pi }^{2}}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{2{\\pi }^{2}}{9}"] ["math" "\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}"] ["math" "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{9}"] ["math" "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-29004" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2{\\pi }^{2}}{9}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-29005" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=-1$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi .$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-29005" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},$ therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-1.\n$$\n\nNow, we have to find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-1)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\nNote that $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is not in the range $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ To get the first solution that's within the required range, we need to find an angle that's coterminal with $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ To do this, we simply add $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2\\pi =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\nAs this is a quadrantal angle, to find the remaining solutions we use our knowledge of the sine function. We know that the sine function has its minimum values at:\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi \n$$\n\nSince the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi $ is one period, and we have already found one minimum value in this period, we conclude that $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ is the only solution." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-29005" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f6c1fdf246bff5ae7680fa49922f6b12948c74816459e66df46444dda9aed443"} {"question" "q-29005" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-29005" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}"] ["math" "x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}"] ["math" "x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}"] ["math" "x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}"] ["text" "The equation has no solution."]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-29005" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-338585" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Expressed in **ascending order** in degrees, rounded to one decimal place, the solutions to the equation  $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=-\\frac{4}{3},$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$   are\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘},\\,x≈$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-338585" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{3}{4})={(-48.590\\,377…)}^{∘}≈-{48.5904}^{∘}\n$$\n\nThis value is outside the required domain. To get a value inside the range, we add ${360}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-{48.5904}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}={311.4096}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSo,  our first solution is ${x}_{1}={311.4096}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={48.5904}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={48.5904}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/assets/d319d2ea403460a3b4d86c837a560fd5188476c425399145bb911e1414b0a6f5.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{48.5904}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈{228.5904}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are \n\n$$\nx≈{228.6}^{∘},\\,x≈{311.4}^{∘}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-338585" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f6c1fdf246bff5ae7680fa49922f6b12948c74816459e66df46444dda9aed443"} {"question" "q-338585" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-338585" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "228.6"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-338585" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-338585" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "311.4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-338580" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Expressed in **ascending order** in radians, rounded to one decimal place, the solutions to the equation  $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=-\\frac{3}{2},$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$   are\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}, $\\,x≈$\n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-338580" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{2}{3})=-0.729\\,727…≈-0.7297\n$$\n\nThis value is outside the required domain. To get a value inside the range, we add $2\\pi :$ \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-0.7297+2\\pi ≈5.5535\n$$\n\nSo,  our first solution is ${x}_{1}=5.5535.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=0.7297.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=0.7297.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/assets/48d92d2bf48234d7c7aad44936cdefd97f02804383cb7ced2f838661a8c4922a.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +0.7297 \\\\  & ≈3.8713\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are \n\n$$\nx≈3.9,\\,x≈5.6.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-338580" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f6c1fdf246bff5ae7680fa49922f6b12948c74816459e66df46444dda9aed443"} {"question" "q-338580" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-338580" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3.9"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-338580" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-338580" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5.6"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-77696" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=-\\frac{12}{5}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to one decimal place?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-77696" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},$ therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{5}{12}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{5}{12})=-0.429\\,775…≈-0.429\\,8\n$$\n\nThis value is outside the required domain. To get a value inside the range, we add $2\\pi .$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-0.429\\,8+2\\pi =5.853\\,385…≈5.853\\,4\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=0.429\\,8.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=0.429\\,8.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/assets/912225edc2b860674da7531597fc905a1a9c821eda705dbdcfafdce32385839b.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +0.429\\,8 \\\\  & ≈3.571\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}≈5.853\\,4\\cdot 3.571\\,4=20.9\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-77696" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f6c1fdf246bff5ae7680fa49922f6b12948c74816459e66df46444dda9aed443"} {"question" "q-77696" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-77696" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1.5"] ["math" "13.7"] ["math" "20.9"] ["math" "3.6"] ["math" "6.1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-77696" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "20.9"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-120556" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=-6$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to one decimal place?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-120556" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},$ therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{1}{6})=-0.167\\,448…≈-0.167\\,4\n$$\n\nThis value is outside the required domain. To get a value inside the range, we add $2\\pi .$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-0.167\\,4+2\\pi =6.115\\,785…≈6.115\\,8\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=0.167\\,4.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=0.167\\,4.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/assets/f6f08d799cd2d2e80494d94cc3b18a61f73227b88b25d79c926d65e161d4ce3a.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +0.167\\,4 \\\\  & ≈3.309\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=6.115\\,8\\cdot 3.309\\,0=20.2\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-120556" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f6c1fdf246bff5ae7680fa49922f6b12948c74816459e66df46444dda9aed443"} {"question" "q-120556" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-120556" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "18.3"] ["math" "19.5"] ["math" "20.2"] ["math" "21.6"] ["math" "22.4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-120556" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "20.2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-120548" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=3$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to the nearest integer?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-120548" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},$ therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{1}{3})=(19.471\\,2…)^{∘}≈{19.471}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince ${19.471}^{∘}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={19.471}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={19.471}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={19.471}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/assets/05f0e5d6558c90bf467284d6b0ef17e7cfb546873bcf978e3d0cd8872c1f4436.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{19.471}^{∘} \\\\  & ={160.529}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}≈19.471\\cdot 160.529=3\\,126\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-120548" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f6c1fdf246bff5ae7680fa49922f6b12948c74816459e66df46444dda9aed443"} {"question" "q-120548" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-120548" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\,986"] ["math" "3\\,126"] ["math" "3\\,567"] ["math" "3\\,874"] ["math" "4\\,123"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-120548" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3\\,126"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-77788" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\sqrt{2}$ for $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-77788" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},$ therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we have to find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ lies in the required domain, the first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/assets/a45a91dfd90e1fe477d562486d641e4932722ca62645c3eb4024c4f851c964d3.png)\n\nSince the second solution lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{4}=\\frac{3{\\pi }^{2}}{16}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-77788" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f6c1fdf246bff5ae7680fa49922f6b12948c74816459e66df46444dda9aed443"} {"question" "q-77788" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-77788" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{3\\pi }{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{3{\\pi }^{2}}{16}"] ["math" "\\frac{3{\\pi }^{2}}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{4\\pi }{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{16}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-77788" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3{\\pi }^{2}}{16}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-29938" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{5}{4}$ for $-{180}^{∘}\\le x<{180}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to the nearest integer?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-29938" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},$ therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nNow, we have to find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{4}{5})=(53.130\\,1…)^{∘}≈{53.130}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince ${53.130}^{∘}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={53.130}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={53.130}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={53.130}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/assets/605af77803fb027c09833b50801fe4b8e6a42a2b623af94a732d0f2f222f9f4a.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{53.130}^{∘} \\\\  & ={126.870}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x={53.130}^{∘},{126.870}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}≈53.130\\cdot 126.870≈6\\,741.\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-29938" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f6c1fdf246bff5ae7680fa49922f6b12948c74816459e66df46444dda9aed443"} {"question" "q-29938" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-29938" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1\\,764"] ["math" "4\\,176"] ["math" "4\\,746"] ["math" "6\\,741"] ["math" "7\\,641"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-29938" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6\\,741"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-338802" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Expressed in **ascending order** in degrees, the exact solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=4\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-2\\sqrt{3},$ for $-{180}^{∘}\\le x<{180}^{∘},$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘},\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-338802" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rearrange the given equation and isolate $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =4\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ -3\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},$ therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{3}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nThen, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})={60}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince ${60}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={60}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fouth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/assets/fd0cf34edebb21a059d60bb3588b27df2da4c86de0bdca044c0e702d9e239d7b.png)\n\nSince the second solution lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{60}^{∘} \\\\  & ={120}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to the equation, in ascending order, are \n\n$$\nx={60}^{∘},\\,x={120}^{∘}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-338802" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f6c1fdf246bff5ae7680fa49922f6b12948c74816459e66df46444dda9aed443"} {"question" "q-338802" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-338802" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "60"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-338802" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-f