R}={60}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/assets/4cb8f27f6c108091d233ec0f2a2dbb3439c7df93747917e6a50db770642e76f8.png)\n\nSince the second solution lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{60}^{∘} \\\\  & ={120}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to the equation, in ascending order, are \n\n$$\nx={60}^{∘},\\,x={120}^{∘}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-274989" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f6c1fdf246bff5ae7680fa49922f6b12948c74816459e66df46444dda9aed443"} {"question" "q-274989" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-274989" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "60"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-274989" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-274989" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "120"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-338798" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Expressed in **ascending order** in radians, the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=-\\sqrt{2}$ for $-2\\pi \\le x<0,$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-338798" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we have to find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\text{arcsin}(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/assets/012f0986293e2a4318dc68633a4ac0e91732d74300496427aaaa6635b417efe4.png)\n\nSince the second solution lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}$ is not in the required domain $-2\\pi \\le x\\le 0.$ To get a solution that is inside the required domain, we subtract $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}-2\\pi =-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order,  the solutions are \n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,x=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-338798" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f6c1fdf246bff5ae7680fa49922f6b12948c74816459e66df46444dda9aed443"} {"question" "q-338798" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-338798" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{3\\pi}{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-338798" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-338798" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{\\pi}{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-29095" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following equations has no solutions for $0\\le x<2\\pi ?$\n\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=10$\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}$\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=-1$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-29095" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that the range of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, only equation II has no solutions." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-29095" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f6c1fdf246bff5ae7680fa49922f6b12948c74816459e66df46444dda9aed443"} {"question" "q-29095" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-29095" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] 