eference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-120609" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f6c1fdf246bff5ae7680fa49922f6b12948c74816459e66df46444dda9aed443"} {"question" "q-120609" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-120609" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-120609" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-66005" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following equations has no solutions for $0\\le x<2\\pi ?$\n\n- $4\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+1=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+10=2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-6$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-66005" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We first simplify the given equations, and get\n\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{3}$\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=10$\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=6$\n\nNow, recall that the range of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, only equation I has no solutions." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-66005" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f6c1fdf246bff5ae7680fa49922f6b12948c74816459e66df46444dda9aed443"} {"question" "q-66005" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-66005" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-66005" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-120608" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following equations has no solutions for $0\\le x<2\\pi ?$\n\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=-2$\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{4}$\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=-\\frac{3}{5}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-120608" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that the range of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, only equations II and III have no solutions." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-120608" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f6c1fdf246bff5ae7680fa49922f6b12948c74816459e66df46444dda9aed443"} {"question" "q-120608" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-120608" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "q-120608" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/state.json" "file_sha256" "17040495ac96a9b080b7103662ab31bf6e714c4afbd68d91c872f37ab48f4ff7"} {"question" "e-3533" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the equation $2\\csc x+4=0$ for $0 < x <   2\\pi.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/example-3533.json" "file_sha256" "012bc77f977997f79c16f03808ec9da507ba5912624355de3d09709d27f0bb71"} {"question" "e-3533" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\csc {x} \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+4 & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-4 \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\csc{x} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin{x}}.$ Therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\sin{x} = \\dfrac{1}{(-2)} = -\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx = \\arcsin\\left(-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right) = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nThis value is outside the required domain $0 < x <   2\\pi.$ To get a value inside the required domain, we add $2\\pi\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\nx_1 = x + 2\\pi = -\\dfrac \\pi 6 + 2\\pi = \\dfrac{11\\pi}6\n$$\n\nSo,  our first solution is $x_1 = \\dfrac{11\\pi}6.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the  fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of $\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}.$ Since $\\sin{x}<    0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle $\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/assets/85b7b73fa793153cfedb2ffdb6825edd0845f93dbb472404862a78476a56fc35.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=\\dfrac{7\\pi}{6}, \\dfrac{11\\pi}{6}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/example-3533.json" "file_sha256" "012bc77f977997f79c16f03808ec9da507ba5912624355de3d09709d27f0bb71"} {"question" "e-4262" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find, in radians to $1$ decimal place, all of the solutions to the equation $\\dfrac{\\csc x}{5} -1 = 0$ for $0 \\leq x <              2\\pi.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/example-4262.json" "file_sha256" "563af5434678653c1053213abc67b6354e44d2ba1071e5b0a189c8a6ab372c53"} {"question" "e-4262" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\csc {x} \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡x}{5}-1 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡x}{5} & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\csc{x} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin{x}},$ therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\sin{x} = \\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{1}{5})=0.201\\,357…≈0.201\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $0.201$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is $x_1 = 0.201.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of $\\theta_R = 0.201.$ Since $\\sin{x} > 0,$ the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has a reference angle $\\theta_R = 0.201.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/assets/8bcd1ea412f9235a330f37ee9059a023d0ce9e03e2c38388875dc0c12afebc1e.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -0.201 \\\\  & ≈2.941\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions (rounded to one decimal place) are $x = 0.2, 2.9.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/example-4262.json" "file_sha256" "563af5434678653c1053213abc67b6354e44d2ba1071e5b0a189c8a6ab372c53"} {"question" "e-3535" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the equation $2\\csc x + 4 = 0$ for $0 < x \\leq   \\dfrac{7\\pi}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/example-3535.json" "file_sha256" "70d5b705e754bb2e52be21d8374439e5e6b65effc20b27f31521363e47f9e4b8"} {"question" "e-3535" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\csc {x} \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+4 & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-4 \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\csc{x} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin{x}}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\sin{x} = \\dfrac{1}{(-2)} = -\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx = \\arcsin\\left(-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right) = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nThis value is outside the required domain. To get a value inside the required domain, we add $2\\pi.$\n\n$$\nx_1 = x+2\\pi = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{6} + 2\\pi = \\dfrac{11\\pi}6.\n$$\n\nSo, our first solution is $x_1 = \\dfrac{11\\pi}{6}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the  fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of $\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}.$ Since $\\sin{x}<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle $\\theta_R =\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/assets/85b7b73fa793153cfedb2ffdb6825edd0845f93dbb472404862a78476a56fc35.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo get the remaining solutions in the given interval, we need to find angles that are coterminal with $x_1$ or $x_2.$ To do this, we add integer multiples of $2\\pi$ until all solutions are found:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3} & ={x}_{1}+2\\pi =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}+2\\pi =\\frac{23\\pi }{6} \\\\ {x}_{4} & ={x}_{2}+2\\pi =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}+2\\pi =\\frac{19\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\dfrac{23\\pi}{6}$ is outside the required domain, so we ignore it.\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=\\dfrac{7\\pi}{6},\\dfrac{11\\pi}{6}, \\dfrac{19\\pi}{6}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/example-3535.json" "file_sha256" "70d5b705e754bb2e52be21d8374439e5e6b65effc20b27f31521363e47f9e4b8"} {"question" "e-4263" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the equation $\\csc{x} = \\dfrac{2}{5}$ for $-2\\pi \\leq x <           2\\pi.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/example-4263.json" "file_sha256" "47a6d132b603c25ce1f2d2b2ad0d3603313c4d3637b18a36d18d9122544e899e"} {"question" "e-4263" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that $\\csc{x} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin{x}}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\sin{x} =\\dfrac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe equation above has no solution because the sine function always satisfies $-1 \\leq \\sin{x} \\leq 1,$ and the value $\\dfrac{5}{2}$ is outside of this range.\n\nTherefore, the equation $\\csc{x} = \\dfrac{2}{5}$ has no solution." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/example-4263.json" "file_sha256" "47a6d132b603c25ce1f2d2b2ad0d3603313c4d3637b18a36d18d9122544e899e"}] "usage" {"e-4262" [] "q-29095" [] "e-3535" [] "q-29004" [] "e-4263" [] "e-3533" []} "basis" 837 "retractions" [[17592186115620 :question/problem "> A calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nGiven that $x_{1}$ and $x_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\csc x = \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ for $0 \\le x < 2π$, what is the value of $x_{1} \\cdot x_{2}$?"] [17592186115623 :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions for $0 \\le x < 2π$?\n\n1. $\\csc x = 10\\frac{1}{2}$\n2. $\\csc x = \\frac{1}{2}\\frac{1}{2}$\n3. $\\csc x =-1\\frac{1}{2}$"] [17592186114925 :question/problem "Solve the equation $2\\csc x+4=0$ for $0 < x < 2\\pi$."] [17592186114925 :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\csc {x}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\csc x + 4 &= 0 \\\\\n2\\csc x &= -4 \\\\\n\\csc x &= -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that\n\n$$\n\\csc{x} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin{x}}\n$$\n\nTherefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\sin{x} = \\dfrac{1}{(-2)} = -\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx = \\arcsin\\left(-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right) = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nThis value is outside the required domain $0 < x < 2\\pi$. To get a value inside the required domain, we add $2\\pi$:\n\n$$\nx_1 = x + 2\\pi = -\\dfrac \\pi 6 + 2\\pi = \\dfrac{11\\pi}6\n$$\n\nSo, our first solution is\n\n$$\nx_1 = \\dfrac{11\\pi}6\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nSince $\\sin{x}< 0$, the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1566/Source/Images/dad47ac6e5b2ff506997d004ecfa60e6.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{2} &= π + θ_{R} \\\\\n&= π + \\frac{π}{6} \\\\\n&= \\frac{7π}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{7\\pi}{6}, \\dfrac{11\\pi}{6}\n$$"] [17592186114968 :question/problem "Find, in radians to $1$ decimal place, all of the solutions to the equation $\\dfrac{\\csc x}{5} -1 = 0$ for $0 \\leq x < 2\\pi$."] [17592186114968 :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\csc {x}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\csc x}{5} - 1 &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{\\csc x}{5} &= 1 \\\\\n\\csc x &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that\n\n$$\n\\csc{x} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin{x}}\n$$\n\ntherefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\sin{x} = \\frac{1}{5}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx = \\arcsin (\\frac{1}{5}) = 0.201357… \\approx 0.201\n$$\n\nSince $0.201$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is\n\n$$\nx_1 = 0.201\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = 0.201\n$$\n\nSince $\\sin{x} > 0$, the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has a reference angle\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = 0.201\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1566/Source/Images/ed103d88ba3ff90d3c2bd12986afbef5.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{2} &= π - θ_{R} \\\\\n&= π - 0.201 \\\\\n&\\approx 2.941\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions (rounded to one decimal place) are\n\n$$\nx = 0.2, 2.9\n$$"] [17592186114926 :question/problem "Solve the equation $2\\csc x + 4 = 0$ for $0 < x \\leq \\dfrac{7\\pi}{2}$."] [17592186114926 :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\csc {x}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\csc x + 4 &= 0 \\\\\n2\\csc x &= -4 \\\\\n\\csc x &= -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that\n\n$$\n\\csc{x} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin{x}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\sin{x} = \\dfrac{1}{(-2)} = -\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx = \\arcsin\\left(-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right) = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nThis value is outside the required domain. To get a value inside the required domain, we add $2\\pi$.\n\n$$\nx_1 = x+2\\pi = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{6} + 2\\pi = \\dfrac{11\\pi}6\n$$\n\nSo, our first solution is\n\n$$\nx_1 = \\dfrac{11\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nSince $\\sin{x}<0$, the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle\n\n$$\n\\theta_R =\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1566/Source/Images/1ea3d46403714f4a6fd27389cf88708d.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{2} &= π + θ_{R} \\\\\n&= π + \\frac{π}{6} \\\\\n&= \\frac{7π}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo get the remaining solutions in the given interval, we need to find angles that are coterminal with $x_1$ or $x_2$. To do this, we add integer multiples of $2\\pi$ until all solutions are found:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{3} &= x_{1} + 2π = \\frac{11π}{6} + 2π = \\frac{23π}{6} \\\\\nx_{4} &= x_{2} + 2π = \\frac{7π}{6} + 2π = \\frac{19π}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{23\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nis outside the required domain, so we ignore it.\n\nTherefore, the solutions are\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{7\\pi}{6},\\dfrac{11\\pi}{6}, \\dfrac{19\\pi}{6}\n$$"] [17592186114969 :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\csc{x} = \\dfrac{2}{5}$ for $-2\\pi \\leq x < 2\\pi$."] [17592186114969 :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\csc{x} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin{x}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\sin{x} =\\dfrac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nThe equation above has no solution because the sine function always satisfies\n\n$$\n-1 \\leq \\sin{x} \\leq 1\n$$\n\nand the value\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nis outside of this range.\n\nTherefore, the equation\n\n$$\n\\csc{x} = \\dfrac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nhas no solution."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186113121 17592186113122 17592186113123 17592186113124 17592186113125 17592186113139 17592186113140 17592186113141 17592186113142 17592186113143] "immutable_fields" [[17592186113120 :answer-field/correct] [17592186113120 :answer-field/id] [17592186113120 :answer-field/key] [17592186113120 :answer-field/type] [17592186113138 :answer-field/correct] [17592186113138 :answer-field/id] [17592186113138 :answer-field/key] [17592186113138 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
